# Git commit message format

When the user **does** ask for a commit, use this **subject line** shape (one line, **72 characters or fewer** when practical):

```text
<TICKET> <TYPE>: <short imperative description>
```

## Parts

- **`<TICKET>`** — Issue id from context (e.g. `ATC-2363`). Prefer the branch name (`ATC-2363-FEATURE-…`) or what the user/Jira ticket states. If unknown, ask once instead of inventing a number.
- **`<TYPE>`** — Short uppercase category, e.g. `FEATURE`, `FIX`, `REFACTOR`, `MODEL`, `DEV ENV`, `INTERNAL`, `DOCS`, `TEST`. Match types already used on the branch when obvious.
- **`<description>`** — Imperative, specific, no trailing period unless it is a full sentence the team already uses.

### Subject line examples

```text
ATC-2363 FEATURE: Activity History - Telemarketer Dropdown
ATC-2363 DEV ENV: Add telemarketer seeds
ATC-2363 REFACTOR: Extract quote activity history edit policy load
ATC-2363 MODEL: Add telemarketer associations on computed and fact models
```

**Do not** switch to unrelated formats (e.g. Conventional Commits `feat:` only) unless the user explicitly requests that repo’s alternate convention for this task.

## Line width

- **72 characters** maximum per line for the subject and for every body line (including wrapped continuation lines of a bullet).
- Wrap long bullets at word boundaries when a line would exceed 72 characters.

## Brevity

- For **commit messages** and for **summaries of a commit** in chat, treat the **subject as the whole story** when it can stand alone.
- For types other than **FIX**, avoid multi-paragraph prose unless the user asks for more depth.
- If you need more than the subject, prefer a short **body** (see below)—about **3–6 tight bullets** of what changed, not narrative—unless the commit is a **bug fix** (see **FIX commits**).

## Body (optional)

- Add a **blank line** after the subject, then **bullets** starting with `- `.
- Each bullet: **imperative**, narrow scope (area or file + behavior). No trailing period unless a full sentence is required.
- **Omit the body** when the subject is enough; keep the subject line compliant with the format above and within the **line width** limit.

When wrapping up right after a commit, mirror this style (**subject + optional body**). For **FIX** commits, a structured PR-style body is preferred when the fix is non-trivial (see below).

## FIX commits (bug fixes)

When **`<TYPE>`** is **`FIX`**, use the **subject** format unchanged, then a **blank line**, then a body written like a **short PR description** so reviewers see **symptom**, **why**, and **what changed**.

Use **section headers** on their own line (no markdown `##` in the commit—plain text words only), each followed by a blank line, then wrapped prose or bullets at **72 columns**:

1. **`Problem`** — What was wrong for the user or system (symptom, failing behavior, or the test/contract that broke). Not a dump of stack traces.
2. **`Root cause`** — Why it happened; narrow and technical enough to be actionable.
3. **`Fix`** — What you changed; use `- ` bullets if several files or behaviors; imperative, scoped.
4. **`Verification`** — How you proved it: test command(s), manual check, or “covers scenario X”. One line is fine if obvious.

**Omit a section** only when it would be empty or redundant (e.g. root cause equals fix in a one-liner fix).

Same **line width** rule: wrap all body lines to **72 characters**. Skip trailers such as `Made-with:` unless the user or repo tooling requires them.

## Examples

### Subject-only (any type)

```text
ATC-2423 FIX: Null-safe quote lookup when policy number missing
```

### FIX with PR-description body

```text
ATC-2423 FIX: Stabilize lead_activity tests with Time.zone locals

Problem

Lead activity system tests froze time with DateTime.new(...) and assumed
the app’s calendar day and lead-activity windows would line up with those
moments. Under config.time_zone (Central Time), that assumption was wrong.

Root cause

In Ruby, DateTime.new without a zone uses offset +00:00. travel_to anchored
“now” in UTC while Time.zone and dashboards use the application zone, so
frozen “today” and ranking windows diverged.

Fix

- travel_to and quick_import dates: Time.zone.local(...) for the same
  instants.
- Manual leads: explicit next-day received_date where counts must stay out
  of window.

Verification

bin/rails test test/system/importers/lead_activity_test.rb
```
